course: dictionary
lesson: 9549
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. The word «stopping» can mean coming to an end, making something stop, or staying somewhere for a short time. Context tells you which one is right.
explain: «The bus is stopping.» means the bus is coming to a halt and no longer moving. «We are stopping at Gran's tonight.» means we are staying there for a short time. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
explain: «Stopping, breathing slowly and counting to ten before you speak or act helps you calm down.» Here stopping means pausing what you are doing for a moment, so you can think before you act.
fact: Stopping can mean coming to an end or no longer moving.
fact: Stopping can mean making someone or something stop, as in stopping the ball.
fact: Stopping can mean staying somewhere for a short time, as in «We are stopping at Gran's tonight.»
fact: The Highway Code gives a stopping distance at 30 mph of about 23 m: about 9 m thinking plus 14 m braking.
fact: Useful phrases include stopping distance, non-stop and stopping by.
exercise: «The bus is stopping at the corner.» What does stopping mean here? | Making the bus go faster | *Coming to a halt, no longer moving | Staying at Gran's for the night | Painting the bus red
exercise: «We are stopping at Gran's tonight.» What does stopping mean here? | Coming to an end for good | Kicking a ball back to the goal | *Staying somewhere for a short time | Stopping someone else from speaking
exercise: The Highway Code gives a stopping distance at 30 mph of about 23 m, made of about 9 m thinking plus braking. How much of that is braking? | About 9 m | About 23 m | *About 14 m | About 30 m
test: «Stopping, breathing slowly and counting to ten» helps you do what? | Shout louder | Run faster | *Calm down | Fall asleep at once
test: Which sentence uses stopping to mean making something stop? | «We are stopping at Gran's tonight.» | *«Stopping the ball is hard when you are tired.» | «The bus is stopping.» | «Stopping by the shop is fun.»
exam: A car needs a long stopping distance, especially on wet or icy roads, so why must a child never step out in front of traffic? | Because cars always stop instantly | *Because the car may not be able to stop in time | Because stopping is only for buses | Because roads are always dry
